The '''Urban Redevelopment Project''' ({{lang-bl|Projecto de Reurbanitzación Urbana}}), popularly known as the '''Casajuan Plan''' ({{lang-bl|Plano Casajuan}}) or '''Casajuan Initiative''' is a set of policy initiatives by the [[Confederal Congress]] with the overarching aim of encouraging {{W|urban renewal}} and reversing {{W|urban decay}} and {{W|suburbanization}} as {{W|remote work}} and {{W|e-commerce}} reduce the need for commuting and office and retail space. An impact assessed plan was developed between 2015 and 2019, in order to determine how to achieve redevelopment of abandoned or vacant office and retail space in {{W|urban centers}} into {{W|mixed-use development}}s in order to alleviate the [[Baliscan housing shortage]]. Its implementation has been tied into the updated [[Low Impact Initiative]] and [[Green Protocol]].

The plan calls for the identification of urban property left vacant for more than two years, which is to be transferred to the control local planning and development boards, which determines the best course of action by assessing community needs and sentiment. As per confederal guidance, all residential conversions must be at least 85% {{W|affordable housing|reserved as affordable}} (meaning [[W:Public ownership|public]] or [[W:Social ownership|social ownership]]) under [[Communes of Balisca|communal]] and [[Cantons of Balisca|cantonal]] [[Housing authority (Balisca)|housing authorities]]. The project is then voted on by the community boards, before making its way up to the commune council and ultimately the cantonal council.

== Background ==
The origins of the Casajuan Plan are rooted in the significant social and environmental challenges of the mid-20th century. From the 1950s onward, [[Castejón]] and other cities saw a period of intense {{W|verticalization}}, with the capital's 50-meter height restriction imposed after the [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake|1924 earthquake]] being relaxed. This encouraged a boom in the construction of increasingly taller high-rise buildings, culminating in the controversial 2018 [[Ampurias Tower]], a mixed-use [[W:Supertall skyscraper|supertall skyscraper]], which came to symbolize a form of development that was often aesthetically jarring and environmentally insensitive. This era of unchecked vertical growth caused increased public concern over its effects on urban ecology, such as changes to air circulation (dubbed as 'wall effect') and light pollution. The controversy surrounding the approval of the Ampurias Tower propelled the movement against continued skyscraper development into the national spotlight, and the seven years since it was completed 50,479 of Balisca's 57,681 [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] have either introduced restrictions on supertalls or banned them entirely. 

In response to the economic slowdown caused by the {{W|Great Recession}}, the confederal government adopted several initiatives aimed at shifting society (where feasible) towards {{W|telecommuting}}. During the [[w:COVID-19 pandemic|COVID-19 pandemic]] and subsequent {{W|lCOVID-19 lockdowns|lockdowns}}, near-total economic interruption followed by a {{W|COVID-19 recession|recession}} led to unprecedented drops of office occupancy, and advances in [[W:Industrial automation|automation]] and [[W:Remote work|remote work]]. 

== Provisions and principles ==
Its primary provisions and principles include:

* '''Infill development''': The law mandates and prioritizes "[[W:Infill development|infill development]]" as the primary strategy for urban growth. This is a conscious effort to increase the density and livability of existing urban areas while simultaneously protecting surrounding agricultural land and natural ecosystems from urban sprawl.

* '''Sustainable construction''': The law promotes and incentivizes the use of sustainable building materials, such as [[W:Mass timber|mass timber]], which is central to the recent push for "[[W:Plyscraper|plyscrapers]]" and low-carbon construction. This aligns with Balisca's [[W:Eco-socialism|eco-socialist]] and regenerative economic principles.

* '''Integrated green infrastructure and space''': The law requires new developments to integrate green spaces, such as [[W:Permeable pavement|permeable pavement]] and [[W:Rooftop garden|rooftop gardens]], to combat urban pollution, manage stormwater, and improve air circulation (addressing issues like the "wall effect"). All development that requires clearing undeveloped green space must incorporate a proportional amount of green space into its plan.

* '''Housing and public space''': It guides the development of [[W:Public housing|social housing]] and [[W:Housing cooperative|housing cooperatives]], ensuring that new construction is not driven by private profit but serves the "[[Basic Law: Housing and Shelter|universal right to housing]]" and contributes to the creation of vibrant, communal public spaces.

